<!-- Captured verbatim from a Final Cut FCPXML export (Bulgaria, 2026-08-28),
     where the user dropped Custom LUT on one clip and exported.

     FCPXML HAS NO COLOUR ELEMENT. A LUT is <filter-video> plus an <effect uid>
     that is an FxPlug identifier — FCP-internal, not derivable, and guessing
     one has already cost two failed imports on this project. So is the LUT
     itself: `param name="LUT"` is a base64 ozxml document whose <dataValue>
     encodes the chosen .cube. Neither can be authored from a specification,
     which is why this whole fragment is reused as text.

     WHICH LUT THIS IS: whatever was selected in Final Cut when this was
     captured — the payload identifies it and nothing here names it. To change
     the LUT, capture again; editing this file cannot do it.

     @@MIX@@ is the one authored value: "Mix" is the effect's opacity, 0..1.
     Captured at 0.2157. That IS the "low opacity, golden hour" setting, and it
     is the only knob in here that may be changed without a fresh capture.

     Final Cut also emits <adjust-colorConform> alongside this. That element is
     FCPXML 1.11 and fails the 1.10 DTD this project writes, so it is dropped
     rather than carried through — the same fix as in the title fragment. -->

<effect id="r10" name="Custom LUT" uid="FxPlug:14B39AEF-607D-42DF-98DD-DB3DD345E925"/>

<filter-video ref="r10" name="Custom LUT">
        <data key="effectConfig"></data>
        <param name="LUT" key="3" value=""/>
        <param name="Input" key="100/101" value="0 (Rec. 709)"/>
        <param name="Output" key="100/102" value="0 (Rec. 709)"/>
        <param name="Mix" key="99990" value="@@MIX@@"/>
    </filter-video>
